 To organize and analyze data on Earth's magnetic field in Java, you would first need to import the data into your Java program from a file or database. One common way to do this is by using a CSV (Comma-Separated Values) file. Here's an example of how you could read the data from such a file and calculate the average magnetic field intensity:
```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
public class MagneticFieldAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double totalMagneticIntensity = 0;
        int numDataPoints = 0;

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("magnetic_field_data.csv"))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                String[] tokens = line.split(",");
                double magneticIntensity = Double.parseDouble(tokens[3]);
                totalMagneticIntensity += magneticIntensity;
                numDataPoints++;
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        double averageMagneticIntensity = totalMagneticIntensity / numDataPoints;
        System.out.println("Average magnetic field intensity: " + averageMagneticIntensity + " Tesla");
    }
}
```
This code reads the data from a file named "magnetic_field_data.csv" located in the same directory as the Java file. It then calculates the total magnetic field intensity and number of data points, and finally calculates and prints out the average magnetic field intensity.
To harness and convert Earth's magnetic field into usable electricity using Java is a much more complex task that goes beyond the scope of this question. It involves advanced physics principles and requires specialized hardware such as a magnetometer and power conversion circuits. There are ongoing research efforts in this area, but there isn't any existing Java code that can accomplish this directly.

